  "explanation": {
    "what_it_is": "Biotin (vitamin B7) is a water-soluble B vitamin that acts as a coenzyme in your body's metabolic pathways. It is essential for breaking down fats, carbohydrates, and amino acids into usable energy.",
    "how_it_works": "Biotin attaches to enzymes involved in energy production and the synthesis of fatty acids and glucose. It plays a role in gene expression and cell signaling, which influences inflammation, blood sugar regulation, and gut barrier function. Your body cannot produce biotin, so it must come from food or supplementation. Biotin also supports the health of hair, skin, and nails by strengthening keratin structure, though this is a structural rather than systemic mechanism.",
    "proven_benefits": [
      {
        "benefit": "Inflammation reduction",
        "grade": "Moderate",
        "note": "31 human studies show biotin can help lower inflammatory markers, though the magnitude of effect varies across populations."
      },
      {
        "benefit": "Gut and digestive health",
        "grade": "Moderate",
        "note": "37 human studies indicate biotin supports gut barrier integrity and beneficial microbiota balance in some contexts."
      },
      {
        "benefit": "Blood sugar management",
        "grade": "Moderate",
        "note": "34 human studies suggest biotin may improve insulin sensitivity and help stabilize blood glucose levels, particularly in people with metabolic dysfunction."
      }
    ],
    "promising_benefits": [
      {
        "benefit": "Testosterone and hormone balance",
        "grade": "Preliminary",
        "note": "37 human studies hint at potential hormone-modulating effects, but evidence remains inconsistent and mechanisms are not fully clear."
      },
      {
        "benefit": "Immune function",
        "grade": "Preliminary",
        "note": "Animal and cell-culture studies suggest biotin may enhance immune cell activity, but human clinical evidence is lacking."
      }
    ],
    "emerging_research": "Recent work is exploring biotin's role in epigenetic regulation\u2014how it may influence gene expression without changing DNA sequence\u2014which could explain some of its effects on inflammation and metabolic health. Early studies also suggest potential benefit in autoimmune conditions, but this remains experimental and unproven in humans. More targeted clinical trials are needed to separate marketing claims from genuine therapeutic potential.",
    "caveats": "Biotin is water-soluble and generally well-tolerated with minimal toxicity, even at high doses, since excess is excreted in urine. Quality varies among supplements; look for third-party testing. Biotin can interfere with some lab tests (especially biotin-based immunoassays), so inform your doctor if you take high-dose supplements before blood work.",
